{"name":"Good Earth","hex":"#a09883","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"good","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a person or an animal:\nActing in the interest of what is beneficial, ethical, or moral.","Acting in the interest of what is beneficial, ethical, or moral."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["That is good; an elliptical exclamation of satisfaction or commendation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/good"},{"term":"earth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Earth (“the third planet of the Solar System, upon which humans live”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil.","Any general rock-based material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Good Earth (restaurant chain)","blocks":[{"text":"Good Earth was a natural foods restaurant chain and bakery originally founded in Reno, Nevada, by William Galt and Nancy Galt. Murray Horton approached William to expand his deli into restaurants. Murray and Charlotte Horton worked with the Galts to grow the restaurants in the Bay Area. The Hortons owned Good Earth Restaurants and Bakeries as well as some Good Earth Delis in Palo Alto, San Mateo, Santa Clara, Cupertino and Los Gatos. They owned and operated these restaurants until they retired in the early 2000’s. The Galts sold the concept to General Mills in 1980. After expansion to more than 50 locations across the United States, the concept lost popularity. General Mills converted most of the restaurants into other chain restaurants they were operating, such as Red Lobster and Olive Garden."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good_Earth_(restaurant_chain)","wikidata":"Q5582537"},{"title":"The Good Earth","blocks":[{"text":"The Good Earth is a historical fiction novel by Pearl S. Buck published in 1931 that dramatizes family life in an early 20th-century Chinese village in Anhwei. It is the first book in her House of Earth trilogy, continued in Sons (1932) and A House Divided (1935). It was the best-selling novel in the United States in both 1931 and 1932, won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1932, and was influential in Buck's winning the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1938. Buck, who grew up in China as the daughter of American missionaries, wrote the book while living in China and drew on her first-hand observation of Chinese village life. The realistic and sympathetic depiction of the farmer Wang Lung and his wife O-Lan helped prepare Americans of the 1930s to consider Chinese as allies in the coming war with Japan."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5b/GoodEarthNovel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Good_Earth","wikidata":"Q1218608"},{"title":"Good","blocks":[{"text":"In most contexts, the concept of good denotes the conduct that should be preferred when posed with a choice between possible actions. Good is generally considered to be the opposite of evil. The meaning and etymology of the term and its associated translations among ancient and contemporary languages show substantial variation in its inflection and meaning, depending on circumstances of place and history, or of philosophical or religious context."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good","wikidata":"Q15290"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth","wikidata":"Q2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg/250px-Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg/250px-Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In many Abrahamic religions,  angels are considered to be good beings and are contrasted with demons, who are their evil counterparts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg/500px-Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg/1280px-Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Socrates_Pio-Clemen